 Truth, Master, for 15 years I've worked for you. A long time ago I should have been freed. You know it's not right, Master.

18
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:22,550
 But my goodness, what would become of you without me? You'd have to leave your family forever.

19
00:03:22,870 --> 00:03:26,170
 Here's a home roof over your head, plenty of food to eat here, you're all right.

20
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,760
 And besides, you know you cost me at least one pound a year to clothe and feed.

21
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:35,880
 Don't believe me, you're better off staying here with us.

22
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:37,850
 Yes, I was a certainly fire plantation.

23
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,350
 You think you've been loafing enough?

24
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:44,350
 Get to work now. Go on. Go on.

25
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:46,740
 What are you waiting for? Get to work, lazy.

26
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:50,440
 You're no question about it. Slaves were very useful

27
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:53,940
 for doing the very hard work on the cotton and tobacco plantations

28
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,170
 because the Indians, they're fanatics for liberty

29
00:03:57,170 --> 00:04:01,010
 and they weren't docile enough when they were pressed into service earlier.

30
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,240
 With its rebellious Indians and its black slaves,

31
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:07,180
 the America, the 13 colonies, was looking for its identity,

32
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:08,700
 but it went to extremes sometimes.

33
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:12,910
 so it was that some regretted the lost purity of the pilgrims never forget

34
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:17,320
 sinners the judgment has come on upon us all of you every one of you is a sinner

35
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:21,490
 in the grip of an angry God now I will tell you the terrible torches of hail

36
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:28,290
 that await you sinners know ye there will be no redemption no

37
00:04:28,290 --> 00:04:31,410
 remission in heaven for any who transgress the holy word of the Lord

38
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:35,490
 Almighty repent repent you sinners repent in hell you miserable sinners will

39
00:04:35,490 --> 00:04:39,030
 roast in the depths of the inferno repent all of you repent now before it's

40
00:04:39,030 --> 00:04:41,090
 Before it's too late, what awaits you sinners!

41
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:44,090
 Cast into the fire these wings these jewels can't go!

42
00:04:44,090 --> 00:04:48,090
 Cast into the purifying fire all these things of Satan!

43
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:49,090
 Hallelujah!

44
00:04:49,090 --> 00:04:50,090
 Hallelujah!

45
00:04:50,090 --> 00:04:51,090
 Hallelujah!

46
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,270
 Hallelujah!

47
00:04:53,270 --> 00:05:13,070
 This explosion, this great revival would soon burn out.

48
00:05:13,070 --> 00:05:16,070
 Fortunately, there were more reasonable voices that made themselves heard as well.

49
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:18,070
 Benjamin Franklin, for example.

50
00:05:18,070 --> 00:05:21,350
 If I believe in the existence of God and the immortality of the soul,

51
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:24,350
 my reason tells me that I have no need of any revelation.

52
00:05:24,350 --> 00:05:30,180
 Strange, I have the feeling that I've already known that chap.

53
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,250
 Hmm.

54
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:37,490
 Yes, you could meet the best and the worst in this America.

55
00:05:37,490 --> 00:05:40,190
 In the middle of the 18th century, the country was too new.

56
00:05:40,190 --> 00:05:43,720
 It needed a melting pot to forge itself into a true nation.

57
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:50,610
 Oh, yes, for the present, what was there in common between this rich southern planter

58
00:05:50,610 --> 00:05:54,310
 with his plantation of thousands of acres or a Negro slave

59
00:05:54,310 --> 00:05:57,960
 or this poor northern farmer who has to do everything for himself?

60
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:01,160
 And be sure we remember to cut planks and fix that leaky roof.

61
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:05,520
 Oh, yes, Mary, and you remember to smoke the bacon and, every once in a while, add water to brew the beer.

62
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:10,200
 Oh, yes, you can sure count on it, John, and the wagon. You have to fix the wheel, John, and chew the horse, too.

63
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:13,800
 I know, Mary, but the livestock are looking poor. Give them more fodder.

64
00:06:14,390 --> 00:06:17,500
 Watch your wheel, John, just as soon as you bring in a nice crop of hay.

65
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:22,640
 Yes, Mary, and remember to prepare the leather for my new boots and my wool jacket.

66
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:25,180
 You'd better finish it soon. Nights are getting just a bit nippy.

67
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:30,230
 And we will need wood to burn in the stove. It heats our whole house.

68
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:37,440
 Let us thank the Lord.

69
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,530
 Yes, John, for all his blessings on us.

70
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:46,370
 And what did they have in common, those craftsmen in the cities?

71
00:06:46,370 --> 00:06:48,060
 Hmm?

72
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:53,920
 The merchants, and the shopkeepers.

73
00:06:53,920 --> 00:07:01,290
 And especially, the frontiersmen, who are clearing the land, the pioneers, and more often than not, the adventurers.

74
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:03,100
 What did they all have in common?

75
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:27,840
 Nevertheless, a handful of men tried to draw together all these divergent interests, one notable among them.

76
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:31,130
 Just a minute, I'm sure I know that chap.

77
00:07:31,130 --> 00:07:34,750
 Anyway, in Albany, in the year 1754...

78
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:38,090
 It is essential for us to adopt a common policy concerning the Indians.

79
00:07:38,310 --> 00:07:42,330
 We must build our forts together, and we ourselves are responsible for our defense.

80
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:45,310
 But, Mr. Franklin, the English soldiers defend us very well.

81
00:07:45,370 --> 00:07:48,870
 Yes, possibly, but gentlemen, this way we are like a snake cut up into segments.

82
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:50,090
 The snake cannot live.

83
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:53,810
 And neither can we if we do not unite.

84
00:07:53,950 --> 00:07:54,750
 This we must do.

85
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:56,320
 Unite who with what?

86
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:57,940
 You can only unite things that are alike.

87
00:07:58,020 --> 00:07:58,940
 I'm a Virginian, sir.

88
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:02,140
 I failed to see what I could possibly have to do with people in New England.

89
00:08:02,670 --> 00:08:04,060
 No, it's a radical idea.

90
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:11,540
 And those of us who live in perpetual harmony with the Lord, what would you have us do in company with those slave owners and those unscrupulous merchants?

91
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:15,340
 I'll be united against the Indians, you see. The Indians, they're not such a big problem.

92
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:18,800
 I'm telling you, I speak from experience as a frontiersman. Trash me.

93
00:08:31,230 --> 00:08:36,590
 We give you wonderful gifts. You give us furs.

94
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:43,160
 For this junk, we let you use our land. But now we can hunt no more. Animal gone.

95
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:47,260
 We no want this stuff. We want fire sticks. We shoot animals. No getaway.

96
00:08:47,550 --> 00:08:50,540
 If you want not give us rifle, then we go to French. They give us rifle.

97
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:54,190
 Okay, you're right. Next time we'll bring you fire stitch or rifles, like you say.

98
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:58,970
 But meanwhile, have a drink of whiskey, Chief. It warms a pretty's heart.

99
00:08:59,630 --> 00:09:01,110
 Let's drink a friendship, Big Chief.

100
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:13,350
 I'll give Chief fire water.

101
00:09:17,010 --> 00:09:20,900
 Chief, take another swig.

102
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:31,460
 Dear, you give Chief no fire water. You take it all. Take it all.

103
00:09:33,760 --> 00:10:32,700
 And one they won't forget. I'll be with those savage redskins.

104
00:10:32,950 --> 00:10:35,090
 I quite agree. They never live up to their agreements.

105
00:10:35,230 --> 00:10:36,570
 They're all treacherous savages.

106
00:10:37,170 --> 00:10:40,830
 I would even go so far as to say they're always blind drunk when they make their treatise.

107
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:41,490
 Can it be true?

108
00:10:41,610 --> 00:10:42,390
 Yes, yes, exactly.

109
00:10:42,510 --> 00:10:44,170
 But how can we ever believe what they say?

110
00:10:44,530 --> 00:10:45,870
 All that you say is true.

111
00:10:46,050 --> 00:10:47,190
 Who could put them in this state?

112
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:50,070
 Who would give them alcohol to make them agree to anything?

113
00:10:50,370 --> 00:10:51,770
 Tell me I want to know.

114
00:10:51,770 --> 00:10:56,930
 This has decreed that we settle here.

115
00:10:57,110 --> 00:11:01,230
 If the Indians oppose the great design, we must force them from the lands the Lord has given.

116
00:11:01,670 --> 00:11:03,250
 Blessed be Providence, my brothers.

117
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:10,500
 Splendid doctrine that allowing you to massacre human beings whose souls are worth as much as ours.

118
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:17,980
 Unless you gain riches from the toil of slaves, from their burden, their misery.

119
00:11:23,280 --> 00:11:27,090
 Wars in order to seize what they have, it is pillage and theft.

120
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:30,100
 It is true that commerce is generally founded on deception.

121
00:11:30,970 --> 00:11:34,910
 Only cultivation of the land is virtuous, positive, honest, and above all, blessed by God.

122
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:39,200
 Yeah, there you see, I told you, at that time in America, you found the worst and the best.

123
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:45,420
 But Benjamin Franklin, master of oil, had other passionate interests other than politics and philosophy.

124
00:11:47,350 --> 00:12:08,750
 Say that lightning's only electricity, and I'll prove it.

125
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:15,040
 You take your constitutionals in the rain now?

126
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:18,880
 I'm going to perform an experiment of capital importance.

127
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:23,740
 You're going to be with me, where you can help.

128
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:37,210
 You'll see, my son, you'll see. This experiment will go down in history.

129
00:12:37,210 --> 00:12:49,050
 Be careful, whatever.

130
00:12:49,050 --> 00:12:50,400
 Okay.

131
00:12:50,400 --> 00:13:00,090
 Scientific electricity, son, you'll see soon.

132
00:13:00,090 --> 00:13:01,950
 Okay, I guess I will.

133
00:13:01,950 --> 00:13:07,040
 Son?

134
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:31,810
 See, my son.

135
00:13:31,810 --> 00:13:48,380
 1763. The French have just abandoned their American possessions to the English.

136
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:54,220
 Mrs. Revere. Morning, Paul.

137
00:13:54,220 --> 00:13:54,660
 Good day.

138
00:13:54,660 --> 00:13:55,710
 How's the engraving?

139
00:13:55,710 --> 00:13:59,770
 Oh, Ben Franklin and son, going very well, thank you.

140
00:13:59,770 --> 00:14:02,760
 Look here, Ben. An invention of yours works.

141
00:14:03,770 --> 00:14:06,130
 This way, it doesn't hit my house.

142
00:14:06,230 --> 00:14:08,430
 No, lightning is sent right into the ground.

143
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,090
 Very clever, Ben, your lightning rod.

144
00:14:10,210 --> 00:14:11,250
 Bye, thanks again.

145
00:14:15,310 --> 00:14:29,510
 Do you know it's not allowed to make hats here?

146
00:14:29,710 --> 00:14:30,830
 Huh? What? Not allowed?

147
00:14:30,930 --> 00:14:32,410
 What do you mean, not allowed? You're joking?

148
00:14:33,530 --> 00:14:38,280
 The mysterious art of hat-making is now typically within the purview of the United Kingdom.

149
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:40,500
 Where the enactment of profession has been attained,

150
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:45,070
 it is forbidden to undertake any such manufacture beyond the territorial limits,

151
00:14:45,070 --> 00:14:46,690
 and more especially in America.

152
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:48,410
 Any contravention will be punished.

153
00:14:48,610 --> 00:14:49,750
 I hope you have understood.

154
00:14:50,010 --> 00:14:51,790
 I don't want to see you making hats again.

155
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:55,420
 Well, how do you like that?

156
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:56,780
 Never heard such a thing before.

157
00:14:58,250 --> 00:14:59,050
 Don't you worry.

158
00:14:59,150 --> 00:15:00,090
 They won't get away with this.

159
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:05,100
 What I know, just what you have in those sacks there.

160
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:08,000
 Nothing but sugar, sir.

161
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:08,840
 Sugar, that's all.

162
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:09,660
 Why do you want to know that?

163
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:10,980
 And does it come from England?

164
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:11,880
 Of course not.

165
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,320
 Nobody in England grows sugar.

166
00:15:13,420 --> 00:15:14,080
 It's too cold.

167
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:17,270
 In that case, you mean it's from the Antilles, admit it.

168
00:15:17,270 --> 00:15:17,950
 I don't know.

169
00:15:18,010 --> 00:15:19,230
 To me, sugar, sugar, that's that.

170
00:15:19,290 --> 00:15:21,390
 I am confiscating your whole load of sugar.

171
00:15:21,530 --> 00:15:22,370
 It's all contraband.

172
00:15:22,530 --> 00:15:23,050
 Get down.

173
00:15:23,130 --> 00:15:23,610
 Let's go.

174
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:24,130
 But...

175
00:15:25,530 --> 00:15:26,790
 How do you like that?

176
00:15:29,110 --> 00:15:31,910
 They even took my wagon.

177
00:15:32,090 --> 00:15:33,810
 Now what am I going to do to earn a living?

178
00:15:46,650 --> 00:15:48,320
 Well, there we are now.

179
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:50,520
 They've put a tax on glass, paper, lead, paint.

180
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:51,500
 Next, what is it going to be?

181
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:53,960
 They'll make up enough taxes to squeeze us like lemons.

182
00:15:54,060 --> 00:15:54,760
 Those are cursed English.

183
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,100
 Yes, now I've heard they're sending back our saddlers.

184
00:15:57,200 --> 00:15:58,460
 Now you can't even go west anymore.

185
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:00,820
 It's not fair.

186
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:01,600
 Who would hurt a certain thing?

187
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:04,200
 This time the cup overfloweth, my friends.

188
00:16:04,420 --> 00:16:05,280
 Listen, it's incredible.

189
00:16:05,700 --> 00:16:08,100
 To take effect this day, residents of the 13 colonies

190
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:10,280
 must buy and affix stamps on all newspapers,

191
00:16:10,280 --> 00:16:13,200
 almanacs, maritime documents, playing cards, dice, etc.

192
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:14,390
 Dice as well?

193
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:15,760
 Dice as well.

194
00:16:16,120 --> 00:16:17,660
 There are 50 items on the list.

195
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:18,440
 I repeat, 50.

196
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:20,460
 They've gone raving mad.

197
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:23,480
 We won't pay taxes we haven't voted on.

198
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,820
 No taxation without representation.

199
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:29,380
 No taxation without representation.

200
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:30,530
 Down with the tyrant.

201
00:16:31,510 --> 00:16:36,190
 No taxation without representation.

202
00:16:55,910 --> 00:17:15,160
 Governor, you are hereby ordered to pay a tax of threepence per pound on all the tea you carry or I confiscate your cargo.

203
00:17:15,360 --> 00:17:17,790
 But my company ordered me to transport this tea.

204
00:17:17,870 --> 00:17:19,610
 They didn't tell me I had to pay any tax.

205
00:17:19,610 --> 00:17:21,920
 He'll refuse.

206
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:24,860
 We are the Sons of Liberty. We oppose any such payment.

207
00:17:24,860 --> 00:17:26,150
 It's an unfair tax!

208
00:17:26,150 --> 00:17:29,150
 I would suggest that you keep your opinions to yourself, Mr. Samuel Adams.

209
00:17:29,150 --> 00:17:34,850
 Understand, as a representative of the Crown, I maintain this ship must pay all taxes prescribed by the law.

210
00:17:34,850 --> 00:17:38,780
 Sir, we will see about.

211
00:17:38,780 --> 00:18:55,520
 Cast, now these colonies must either submit or defeat me. Do you understand?

212
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:01,730
 This would-be subject of His Majesty George III dumped him to Boston Harbor to a value of £12,000 sterling.

213
00:19:01,730 --> 00:19:06,330
 In rebellion, we cannot tolerating of His Majesty's ordinances...

214
00:19:06,330 --> 00:19:11,550
 I shall order our troops to take action. Unmask the other rebels and arrest them.

215
00:19:12,150 --> 00:19:15,750
 And straight away, they will be expedited here and we will know how to judge them.

216
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:20,140
 Yes, it is quite true. The British crowned it an intolerable injustice towards us all.

217
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:26,990
 Gentlemen, I am Patrick Henry from Virginia, but I maintain there is now no difference between New York, Pennsylvania, New England.

218
00:19:27,190 --> 00:19:30,550
 We are all Americans. We must unite all together against the Redcoats.

219
00:19:31,970 --> 00:19:34,830
 Gentlemen, here's the resolution submitted to our assembly.

220
00:19:35,210 --> 00:19:38,950
 According to the eternal laws of nature, man has the right to life, liberty, and property.

221
00:19:40,050 --> 00:19:45,670
 These rites have never been ceded to a sovereign, that he should dispose of them without the people's consent.

222
00:19:46,010 --> 00:19:50,000
 Knew that one day, sooner or later, we would join together the segments of that snake.

223
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:55,160
 The English are bristling with activity. We must be prepared for anything.

224
00:19:55,380 --> 00:19:58,440
 We will establish a militia, men ready to fight at a minute's notice.

225
00:19:58,980 --> 00:20:00,680
 Yes, we will call them Minute Men.

226
00:20:01,290 --> 00:20:04,170
 Paul, you will be responsible for training them. We count on you.

227
00:20:04,170 --> 00:20:10,350
 Good night, in your homes at work, you must be ready. Ready to answer the call at a minute's notice.

228
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:13,170
 No more. That's why your rifles should always be loaded.

229
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:19,440
 My dear Susie, I would like to ask you a big favor.

230
00:20:20,590 --> 00:20:22,910
 You think the time is right?

231
00:20:23,170 --> 00:20:24,510
 But what exactly do you want?

232
00:20:24,670 --> 00:20:25,130
 It's this.

233
00:20:25,170 --> 00:20:27,430
 I have to be up and dressed and ready to fight in a minute.

234
00:20:27,550 --> 00:20:28,370
 Tonight, wake me up.

235
00:20:28,430 --> 00:20:30,050
 Say one minute, but only one minute.

236
00:20:35,590 --> 00:20:36,250
 One minute?

237
00:20:36,470 --> 00:20:37,230
 Only one minute?

238
00:20:37,720 --> 00:20:38,140
 Hmm?

239
00:20:46,040 --> 00:21:34,150
